 "title": "Container with Most Water",
 "slug": "container-with-most-water",
 "category": "two-pointers",
 "difficulty": "Medium",
 "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/container-with-most-water/",
 "pattern": "Two pointers moving inward",
 "statement": "`height[i]` is the height of a vertical wall at position `i`. Pick two walls; together with the floor they form a container. Return the largest amount of water any pair of walls can hold. Water can only rise to the **shorter** of the two walls.",
 "example": {
  "input": "height = [1,8,6,2,5,4,8,3,7]",
  "output": "49",
  "why": "Walls at index 1 (height 8) and index 8 (height 7): min(8, 7) × (8 − 1) = 49."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "height = [1,1]",
   "output": "1"
  },
  {
   "input": "height = [4,3,2,1,4]",
   "output": "16"
  },
  {
   "input": "height = [1,2,1]",
   "output": "2"
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "Water held = **width × shorter wall**. Trying every pair of walls is O(n²).",
  "Start with the widest container (both ends). Any other container is narrower, so it can only win by having a taller *shorter wall*.",
  "Therefore always move the pointer at the **shorter** wall inward. Moving the taller one cannot help: the width shrinks and the height is still capped by the shorter wall."
 ],
 "code": "class Solution:\n    def maxArea(self, height: List[int]) -> int:\n        left, right = 0, len(height) - 1\n        max_area = 0\n\n        while left < right:\n            current_area = min(height[left], height[right]) * (right - left)\n            max_area = max(max_area, current_area)\n\n            if height[left] < height[right]:\n                left += 1\n            else:\n                right -= 1\n\n        return max_area",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(n)",
  "timeWhy": "Each step moves one pointer inward, so the two pointers take n − 1 steps in total.",
  "space": "O(1)",
  "spaceWhy": "Only two pointers and two numbers are stored."
 },
